const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Copy editing","serviceType":"Editing service","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/academic-editorial-services/copy-editing-services/","description":"Academic and scientific copyediting at $0.035 a word: grammar, usage, consistency, cross-references and a style sheet you keep.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is copy editing, and how does it differ from proofreading?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A copyedit reviews the manuscript for grammar, punctuation, spelling, syntax, consistency, clarity and style. Proofreading is narrower on purpose: it is the final read for surface error, and it is defined against a previous stage — it assumes somebody has already edited the document.\n\nSo the question is not which is more thorough. It is what has already happened to your manuscript. Nobody has edited it, a colleague aside? You want a copyedit. It has been edited and then revised twice? You want a proofread, and buying a copyedit will not find much more."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What types of documents do you copyedit?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Research manuscripts, academic papers, journal articles, theses, dissertations, grant proposals, abstracts, case reports, case studies, technical reports, book chapters, cover letters and professional documents, across disciplines.\n\nWhat we do not take is software localisation, patents, marketing copy and CV editing. They answer to different standards and belong with people who work to those standards."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will my manuscript still sound like me?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and it is the thing authors ask about most. Editors work on how clearly your ideas arrive, not on whose ideas they are. Voice, argument and the order of your claims are left alone.\n\nYou do not have to take that on trust. Every change comes back tracked and every one of them is rejectable, with a comment wherever the reason is not obvious. If an edit has flattened something you meant, you put it back."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you handle subject-specific terminology?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Your manuscript goes to an editor who works inside its subject area, because terminology is the part a general editor cannot check. Knowing that a word is spelled correctly is not the same as knowing it means something different three journals over.\n\nAgreed terms are recorded on a style sheet and applied across the text, the tables and the figure captions together — which is where inconsistency usually survives a read-through."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you format to my journal or publisher’s requirements?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Reference style at its current edition, headings, abbreviations, numbers, units, declarations and limits, checked against that journal’s own instructions rather than against the style it claims to follow — the two differ more often than you would expect.\n\nWhere you are writing for a publisher rather than a journal, we work to their house style sheet if they publish one, and build you one if they do not."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How long does copy editing take?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It depends on length, complexity and subject. A 5,000 to 7,000-word research paper is typically 3 to 5 business days at the Basic package.\n\nThe other packages carry their own turnarounds because they do more, not because they queue longer: 5 to 7 days for Standard, 7 to 10 for Advanced, 10 to 14 for Premium. All of them are on the order before you pay, and a date we have agreed is a date we keep."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will I receive tracked changes and comments?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. The file comes back with every change tracked and a comment wherever the reason is not obvious, along with the style sheet, the query list and a certificate of editing naming the level and the editor.\n\nIt also means you can show a supervisor, a co-author or a journal exactly what an editor did and what they did not — which matters when a declaration is required."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you handle manuscripts in other languages?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The copy editing service is for English-language manuscripts. Where your source is in another language, the work is translation first and copy editing after it, and those are priced as two things because they are two things.\n\nIf that is your situation, start on the translation page instead — the first 500 words are translated free so you can judge the English before committing."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I get started?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Send the manuscript for a quote and a turnaround, or send 1,000 words and we will edit them free, at the level we think you need, so you can see the work on your own text before you buy any of it.\n\nIf the level you asked for is not the level we would recommend, we say so at that point rather than after the invoice."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Editing and Translation","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/academic-editorial-services/copy-editing-services/"}]}];

